WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.500
Rescue, the embassy mission.

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:07.920
A diplomatic mission in a foreign city is seized by political fanatics.

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:31.320
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:33.380
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:34.720
And I'm Joe.

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:37.760
What a colorful way to describe terrorists.

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:39.760
Political fanatics.

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:45.480
I mean, you have to be politically correct about political fanatics.

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:48.780
Yeah, they didn't want to piss off the terrorist audience, you know?

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:50.600
They still wanted to get their money.

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:57.580
And look, I know very little about the 1980s because I wasn't born then, so I don't care to read about what happened.

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:04.240
But I'm pretty sure that this was happening a lot in the 70s and 80s, right?

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:14.560
Like, hostage situations with the Olympic Games in Helsinki and, you know, that's the best I've got for you, so I'm going to just roll with that one as an example.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:17.140
Sounds like you've been doing some research times before you.

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:21.080
No, you know what? I know that one as a weird reason.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:24.560
I was watching a Steve Prefontaine movie.

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:28.500
He's an amazing American track runner who died way too early.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:38.060
And in the great movie that covered his life, as short as it was, that was like one of the things that happened was the hostage situation in Helsinki.

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:44.380
Yeah, I mean, a lot of 80s movies would lead you to believe that this was happening a lot.

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:47.700
I'm sure, like, it did happen a lot.

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:58.140
I don't really know the variation from decade to decade because, you know, one of the most famous examples of it was actually in the 70s.

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:04.120
But I'm sure, you know, every now and then there's some hostages that need to be taken.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:24.460
It just makes you think about the situation in gaming then versus now, because if it really was like a more of a current events thing than ever, now look at, like, Nintendo won't even release Advanced Wars because it's kind of similar to the Russia-Ukraine conflict.

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:26.320
So they're like, no, we can't even release that game.

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:29.800
Meanwhile, Rescue the Embassy mission is like, it's happening all the time.

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:30.920
Let's make a game about it.

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:37.680
Yeah, especially because this is back when I feel like the audience was more kids than it is today.

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:42.200
Yeah, I mean, the game's mechanics lead you to believe it's for kids.

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:45.220
The content, not so much.

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:47.720
Yeah. Yeah, there's blood in this one.

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:48.780
That's interesting, right?

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:51.220
Oh, yeah. We don't usually see blood in Nintendo games.

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:59.120
I thought it was more so you were sort of like tossing an apple into your enemy's chest area.

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:00.760
It's open for interpretation.

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:11.600
Yeah. I don't remember apples as part of the weapon loadout, but, you know, this game, for what it's worth, is kind of like Rainbow Six before Rainbow Six.

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:24.220
It's a game that sets out to do the job that it, you know, like the whole idea of being a six-man team and going around freeing these hosiers from this embassy.

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:32.560
We have seen a few games lately where they don't quite understand the assignment and they focus on one thing in particular.

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:38.340
Like the Infiltrator episode, we kept talking about how the helicopter thing was just way too detailed.

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:49.860
Genghis Khan, another one that like maybe didn't quite focus enough on what it was like to be Genghis Khan and take over the Mongol Empire and more just the same strategy game that we've seen before.

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:55.700
Genghis Khan, the magic of Shahar Azad, you know, like came up with time travel but didn't really fully execute on it.

00:03:56.020 --> 00:04:04.380
This is a game that whether or not it's a good game or not, which is what we'll get into in this episode, it completely understands what kind of game it is.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:16.760
A terrorist group has overrun an embassy in Paris and now it's up to you to, in like three different parts of a mission, set up your snipers, infiltrate the base, and then free all the hostages.

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:18.340
It's simple enough.

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:21.020
It completely stays grounded in that.

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:30.620
It doesn't focus too much on like, okay, well, like, you know, here's how a sniper rifle behaves and like make sure you understand, you know, like make sure you get the timing exactly right.

00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:37.240
Or like here's how a rappelling down a building works and it's incredibly complicated to get inside the building.

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:39.520
It sticks to the assignment.

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:48.960
Do you agree with that? Well, it's interesting because I agree that it feels that way more so than something like Infiltrator.

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:51.980
It feels more cohesive and more like, okay, I understand the mission.

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:58.880
But really when you break it down, it is very similar in the sense that like there's one gameplay part, there's one gameplay element,

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:04.020
and then there's a totally new gameplay element that's introduced halfway through your mission or third of the way through your mission.

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:06.620
And then you do that, and then there's another totally new thing.

00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:10.760
There's three different gameplay elements that have like functionally very little to do with each other.

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:19.920
Like story-wise, it's a progression, but it is like in the same way that Infiltrator is like first you're flying a helicopter and, you know,

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:23.660
talking to enemy helicopters and occasionally fighting with them.

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:28.660
And then suddenly you're on the ground looking through buildings to try and find documents.

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:30.500
That felt very disjointed.

00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:33.480
This is doing a similar thing, but I think it's doing it much better.

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:42.540
Yeah, I mean, so I think what this game is, it's sort of like one of those funhouse games where it's a couple different mini games,

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:47.880
and it's masquerading as a tactics game.

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:54.860
Like, Mike, you brought up the comparison of Rainbow Six because you're like a special tactics unit.

00:05:55.260 --> 00:06:00.660
In this case, I guess like Gigan or whatever, G-I-G-N, the French one.

00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:11.880
It's sort of using the language of like a tactics game where you have to do the setup and then you execute.

00:06:12.860 --> 00:06:15.820
The first one being sort of getting into position.

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:24.920
The second one being getting your guys into the guys that are actually on the roof of the building into position.

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:33.300
And then the snipers sort of taking their shots to prep for the actual extraction of hostages.

00:06:33.500 --> 00:06:36.660
But it's like it's always the same thing.

00:06:36.820 --> 00:06:39.180
Like you don't get to pick where your guys go.

00:06:40.180 --> 00:06:45.180
So you have to go exactly where the game wants you to go.

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:48.400
There's a little interplay between them.

00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:54.820
There is some, like if you take out a window with a sniper and you go to control one of the repellers,

00:06:55.260 --> 00:06:56.620
like that window will be broken.

00:06:56.860 --> 00:07:01.480
But it's not like the enemies inside are responding to it in any way.

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:07.720
So, yeah, I mean, those are my like first impressions of this, like after playing it for a little bit.

00:07:07.720 --> 00:07:15.520
Yeah, that's a good point, Sean, that it's not necessarily a lived in world or even a very interactive world.

00:07:15.620 --> 00:07:24.900
The parts as they're broken out, part one, setting up your snipers, is not something that you have really freedom to explore.

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:29.960
You just have to complete that mission exactly as the game prescribes.

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:38.840
And then you can move on to part two, repelling from the top of the building down into breaking into the windows to get inside.

00:07:39.100 --> 00:07:44.040
And then part three, taking out all of the terrorists in order to free the hostages.

00:07:44.900 --> 00:07:54.240
Those things aren't like live switching between like move your sniper now to another location to get him around and free up another side of the building.

00:07:54.460 --> 00:07:56.220
And then like, OK, now live switch back.

00:07:56.360 --> 00:07:57.620
And I know that sounds crazy, right?

00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:04.860
But technically they are offering that experience of switching between people that are in the building and people that are outside of the building.

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:15.520
It just doesn't really mean that much in the actual game because the game is very focused on only the rules that it's applied to it.

00:08:15.640 --> 00:08:22.400
It's not like the enemies inside the building are so dynamic and live that you have to focus on both elements.

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:27.680
Yeah, Sean, I like what you said, too, about it being like this funhouse game.

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:37.500
Because I was thinking something similar to I felt like each of these sections, like I was playing three separate arcade style games that were like connected narratively.

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:39.540
You know, it's like avoid the lights.

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:45.040
And that's like your game. And you've got the context of like, OK, I'm trying to sneak through, you know, enemy fire or whatever.

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:52.780
And then, you know, the sniper thing is like kind of this search and hold your gun steady little game.

00:08:52.780 --> 00:08:59.880
And then the third one is kind of like a twitch reflex, like a like a zapperless wild gunman over and over again as you kind of go through this building.

00:09:00.080 --> 00:09:06.180
So it's like, yeah, three little mini games that really have no cohesion gameplay wise.

00:09:06.380 --> 00:09:08.940
But they but narratively, that's what draws ties them together.

00:09:09.160 --> 00:09:22.080
But I do think that there's a case for saying that even with the recent streak of 1990s games being multi genre or at least, you know, switching up the control schemes enough to make the game feel different.

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:24.640
This is still kind of surprising.

00:09:25.020 --> 00:09:30.300
I do feel like not too long ago, the whole game would have been, yeah, terrorists have taken over this building.

00:09:30.300 --> 00:09:45.820
And now you're playing a top down action game where you just shoot at, you know, various enemies as you climb up the building to get to the top in like a diehard style video game where at the end, you know, like I'm on the 40th floor is the final boss.

00:09:45.820 --> 00:09:53.100
I think in an alternate universe, this game would have just been like navigating the building.

00:09:53.560 --> 00:09:55.260
There's a lot more assets.

00:09:57.100 --> 00:10:04.840
It's probably the the deepest mechanically of the three phases or four phases, however you want to say it.

00:10:06.020 --> 00:10:18.000
And I mean, it there's enough to hear to say that the getting into position, the snipers and the repelling are kind of like tacked on to this.

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:31.720
Like it's it's not the same kind of multi genre game that we've been seeing a lot of because those have like three or four, two or three phases that are not fleshed out at all.

00:10:31.860 --> 00:10:36.580
Whereas this has two or three phases that aren't fleshed out at all.

00:10:36.700 --> 00:10:39.800
And one phase that is just barely fleshed out.

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:59.940
Let's start where with where the game starts you off, which is well, the game really starts you off with choosing your rank and your mission, which affect the difficulty of the game.

00:11:00.740 --> 00:11:07.880
Rank being like how hard the game is and then mission being the time that you have.

00:11:07.880 --> 00:11:17.720
The game, though, starts you off in the snipers part of the game where you have to bring your three men into position so that they can snipe the building to do so.

00:11:17.860 --> 00:11:26.580
They have to avoid searchlights by diving into doorways or windows or getting on the ground low enough that the searchlights can't see them.

00:11:26.740 --> 00:11:30.200
You can roll, you can run, you can crawl.

00:11:30.380 --> 00:11:32.520
It's it's pretty it's pretty cool.

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:36.660
It's also very short and very limited in the actual experience.

00:11:36.660 --> 00:11:42.460
But if the player is caught in a searchlight, even not not necessarily like it's not an instant death.

00:11:42.740 --> 00:11:46.860
But if you're caught near the searchlight, then the snipers will shoot it.

00:11:47.040 --> 00:11:48.020
Not the snipers.

00:11:48.160 --> 00:11:52.140
Whoever's like artillery is watching you through the searchlights.

00:11:52.240 --> 00:11:53.500
They'll start firing at you.

00:11:53.640 --> 00:12:00.560
And there's a chance that it'll be a death or there's a chance that you'll narrowly escape if you like roll at the right time or everything.

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:05.180
I think there's enough going on here that it's not like I don't know.

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:09.620
It's not it's not a chore to put the snipers into the assigned positions or anything.

00:12:09.640 --> 00:12:11.500
You still have to be on it.

00:12:12.420 --> 00:12:21.540
Yeah, I'd say it's like the least interesting part of the game, though, like just getting to where like you didn't have to walk down the street to get into these buildings.

00:12:21.540 --> 00:12:42.200
Like if this was actually like a tactics situation, you'd have different entrances and that aren't like right at where the enemy can shoot at you, which is also strange because in every other phase of the game, there doesn't seem to be that the enemies don't seem to be aware of anything outside of the building.

00:12:42.200 --> 00:12:45.240
So it's not cohesive in that way either.

00:12:45.860 --> 00:12:51.060
But no, I wouldn't consider it a chore because moving around as these guys is surprisingly fun.

00:12:51.460 --> 00:12:53.560
It just seems unnecessary.

00:12:54.180 --> 00:12:57.660
Yeah, I mean, I actually found this part of the game pretty fun.

00:12:58.440 --> 00:13:01.720
Like compare like I don't know which which part I would say is my favorite.

00:13:01.840 --> 00:13:02.840
But like this was up there.

00:13:03.040 --> 00:13:07.480
I like this a lot. Granted, it's the first thing you play as before I even knew that there was going to be like other segments.

00:13:08.540 --> 00:13:19.900
But, you know, like you said, you move in like a very fluid and like a fun way where you're diving through windows and rolling, you know, to try and avoid the spotlights and everything.

00:13:20.300 --> 00:13:21.340
It feels good.

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:25.620
The only thing I'll say about it is that it it would get old really fast.

00:13:25.640 --> 00:13:30.960
I got to believe, you know, I only played so much of this game, but like it's not difficult, really.

00:13:31.360 --> 00:13:34.480
There's a couple of times you might be like, OK, yeah, this light might get me or whatever.

00:13:34.480 --> 00:13:38.540
What kind of once you get the hang of it or if you just decide to be super cautious, it's not difficult.

00:13:38.760 --> 00:13:44.840
I mean, the only downside to that is you're losing time, but it never it never felt like too much of a challenge.

00:13:45.000 --> 00:13:50.620
So I do think if I like started playing this game a lot or went through all the missions, I'd be I would be tired of it after a while.

00:13:50.820 --> 00:13:53.360
But like in small doses, I thought it was very fun.

00:13:53.360 --> 00:14:14.280
The one thing that I did like about this segment, and it's it's a part of each of them aside from sniping, is that they're the one connective thread between all of these aside from the sort of plot contextual connections is that if you do die.

00:14:14.280 --> 00:14:23.560
And because it's a team of six, if you do die as one of them, like you just don't have access to that to that dude anymore.

00:14:24.080 --> 00:14:34.060
So you'll have a shorthanded team or you'll only have as many snipers at your disposal once you get to the actual infiltration.

00:14:34.380 --> 00:14:36.600
And same goes for repelling.

00:14:37.160 --> 00:14:40.920
But that's a much more goofball way to lose somebody.

00:14:41.160 --> 00:14:43.260
But I did like that.

00:14:43.580 --> 00:14:46.340
Yeah, that is a cool persistent feature.

00:14:46.580 --> 00:14:51.020
And then the other persistent feature is that from the very start of the game, there is a time limit.

00:14:51.420 --> 00:14:57.900
And that is because you presumably only have a certain amount of time before these hostages are are executed.

00:14:57.900 --> 00:14:59.080
So you have to be quick.

00:14:59.100 --> 00:15:02.700
You can change that from anywhere to 20 minutes to 10 minutes.

00:15:03.680 --> 00:15:12.120
And so since the longest possible is 20 minutes, that also makes this a contender for one of the shortest NES games, right?

00:15:14.340 --> 00:15:15.320
I guess so.

00:15:15.560 --> 00:15:20.080
Like the longest the game can be is 20 minutes, but technically there's a mode to beat it in 10.

00:15:20.960 --> 00:15:23.520
I mean, I guess some arcade games are sorry.

00:15:23.640 --> 00:15:25.980
Arcade games could be in the running for that, too.

00:15:25.980 --> 00:15:31.180
But those don't have some semblance of a story and plot.

00:15:31.360 --> 00:15:33.740
So maybe those aren't part of it.

00:15:33.860 --> 00:15:44.840
No, every time Donkey Kong falls and hits his head, he he does get back up and then just steal Pauline all over again to have you go back and start from level one again.

00:15:44.960 --> 00:15:46.820
That it's it's not like the story finished.

