It's like, it's not the first floor, but it's because you don't have access to the ground floor.

00:19:18.660 --> 00:19:23.080
Right. It's the first floor of the three floors that you have access to in the game.

00:19:23.320 --> 00:19:25.480
It's a very European way of thinking about things.

00:19:25.640 --> 00:19:33.440
There you go. You're inside the building now, and now you are switching to, like, a first person.

00:19:33.480 --> 00:19:34.520
Not really, though, right?

00:19:34.520 --> 00:19:36.920
Yeah, I guess most of the time it's first person.

00:19:37.060 --> 00:19:38.440
Yeah. It's first person. Yeah.

00:19:38.480 --> 00:19:40.420
It just looks weird sometimes in the rooms.

00:19:40.820 --> 00:19:43.700
Like, when you're inside the room, then you see yourself.

00:19:43.980 --> 00:19:45.440
Do you? Yeah, right?

00:19:46.140 --> 00:19:47.080
I guess you do.

00:19:47.180 --> 00:19:51.520
Yeah, you're, like, over the shoulder in the rooms, and then in the hallway you just see your gun.

00:19:52.160 --> 00:19:54.640
Yeah, your proportion is very small, though.

00:19:54.820 --> 00:19:58.160
Like, it's very strange. When you're in the hallway, you're just the gun.

00:19:58.240 --> 00:20:03.060
Yes, and I'm not sure why the change for that at all, but, like, I guess, you know,

00:20:03.060 --> 00:20:09.860
they are, like, zoomed in more when it's just the gun, so maybe they thought in the room you had to, like, see more of the room,

00:20:09.900 --> 00:20:11.740
so they switched out to third person.

00:20:11.940 --> 00:20:17.040
All that to say that, you know, if it didn't, if Sean didn't even notice, it's not really that jarring of a feature either.

00:20:17.040 --> 00:20:21.080
It's just changing the view mode for us rather than us controlling the camera.

00:20:21.080 --> 00:20:28.160
The one thing that you have to applaud this game for is that, yes, it is a first-person game where you have to actually, like,

00:20:28.840 --> 00:20:35.360
press the left and right buttons just to turn your body, not even, like, turn to the next rooms or anything like that.

00:20:35.400 --> 00:20:36.240
You're just turning your body.

00:20:36.500 --> 00:20:43.580
There is a map in the left-hand side of the screen that really spells out for you with an arrow which way you're facing

00:20:43.580 --> 00:20:50.500
and the entire floor plan, so this way it's very clear where people could be, where you are, and where the rooms are.

00:20:50.620 --> 00:20:55.700
I really appreciated this, and I feel like all the other first-person games that we've played so far

00:20:55.700 --> 00:20:57.380
should have had this feature.

00:20:57.780 --> 00:21:05.200
Yeah, it's also a very necessary feature with the way that they will spawn these guys.

00:21:05.360 --> 00:21:10.440
Like, when you're on the easier difficulties, they'll actually show on a mini-map where enemies are,

00:21:10.440 --> 00:21:18.440
but that'll also show you that the way that this game populates the map is very strange

00:21:18.440 --> 00:21:23.860
because you'll walk into a room and it'll look empty, but you know that there's a guy in there.

00:21:24.460 --> 00:21:30.460
So you'll have to turn around entirely to realize that in that scene transition,

00:21:30.460 --> 00:21:33.260
you totally missed an enemy in the room.

00:21:33.860 --> 00:21:37.080
Because sometimes, yeah, you'll have to turn around and he'll be there.

00:21:37.080 --> 00:21:41.980
So when you're sort of combing through this place trying to find all the enemies,

00:21:42.160 --> 00:21:45.540
you have to make sure that you did a full sweep of each room.

00:21:46.060 --> 00:21:52.900
Yeah, and as far as I can tell, if the enemy is behind you or to your right and you haven't turned to see him,

00:21:53.440 --> 00:21:54.900
I don't think he'll shoot you, right?

00:21:55.720 --> 00:21:57.200
I don't know. I don't think so.

00:21:57.540 --> 00:22:02.480
Unless I just never waited long enough, or maybe I just never played on a high enough difficulty.

00:22:02.480 --> 00:22:05.020
But there have been times where I'm like, okay, there's definitely a guy here,

00:22:05.200 --> 00:22:07.840
and I waited a significant amount of time, and then I turned and shot him.

00:22:07.900 --> 00:22:10.500
And I was like, I guess he can't see me unless I can see him or something.

00:22:11.240 --> 00:22:12.580
Or they're just very polite.

00:22:13.160 --> 00:22:18.260
Maybe, yeah. It's dishonorable to shoot someone when their back is turned.

00:22:18.620 --> 00:22:22.860
Yeah, I'm fairly certain that they do have to be on the same screen as you,

00:22:22.920 --> 00:22:25.160
that they can't just shoot you from within the same room.

00:22:25.160 --> 00:22:30.760
But that is kind of funny to think that you entered a room, and you're just looking straight ahead,

00:22:30.880 --> 00:22:36.480
but this guy is looking straight ahead at a wall, not the door that you entered from.

00:22:37.040 --> 00:22:41.580
Realistically, if they're inside this building, they should only be looking for the doors, right?

00:22:42.620 --> 00:22:44.860
Yeah. No one's coming in through the walls.

00:22:45.820 --> 00:22:52.480
What I think is really probably happening is that your character is wearing bulletproof armor

00:22:52.480 --> 00:22:54.820
all around his body except for his front.

00:22:55.400 --> 00:22:58.060
Yeah, that's what I would do. So they can only shoot at you if you're facing them.

00:22:58.560 --> 00:23:05.540
I mean, but we also have drawn the context that we have been shooting at this building from nearby buildings,

00:23:05.540 --> 00:23:09.820
so maybe they haven't heard you shooting up the third floor,

00:23:10.400 --> 00:23:13.220
and they're still worried about the snipers across the street.

00:23:13.440 --> 00:23:17.480
And also, it just becomes a game of quick draw when you do see them.

00:23:17.480 --> 00:23:22.680
It's just make sure you shoot really fast the second you see anybody green on your screen

00:23:22.680 --> 00:23:25.500
because that's how you take care of enemies.

00:23:25.500 --> 00:23:30.540
If you stand too still or are surprised by the enemy, you'll get shot at,

00:23:30.620 --> 00:23:32.180
and that's no good for you.

00:23:32.320 --> 00:23:34.580
So just shoot as fast as you can.

00:23:34.700 --> 00:23:36.800
There's really not much else to the—

00:23:36.800 --> 00:23:39.840
there's not like an aiming component necessarily, right?

00:23:40.100 --> 00:23:42.960
Not at all. And, you know, I also felt—

00:23:42.960 --> 00:23:45.040
and granted, maybe I should have played some harder difficulties.

00:23:45.040 --> 00:23:48.280
I mean, I only played, like, the first two—I think the first two difficulties.

00:23:48.660 --> 00:23:50.040
But does it ever get hard?

00:23:50.680 --> 00:23:52.500
I mean, it feels like—

00:23:52.500 --> 00:23:56.520
it feels like you have, like, a good two seconds to shoot before they shoot you.

00:23:56.740 --> 00:23:59.100
As soon as you see them, you just press the shoot button.

00:23:59.220 --> 00:24:00.520
You have plenty of time.

00:24:00.720 --> 00:24:01.740
It's not like—

00:24:01.740 --> 00:24:02.700
it's not like—

00:24:02.700 --> 00:24:05.340
I'm even thinking of, like, Wild Gunman or—

00:24:05.880 --> 00:24:07.940
what is the one where you're training for the FBI, whatever,

00:24:08.060 --> 00:24:09.200
the Zapper games where it's like,

00:24:09.800 --> 00:24:12.320
there's things that make the twitch reflexes harder.

00:24:12.320 --> 00:24:15.020
There's things like, oh, don't shoot the innocent person.

00:24:15.280 --> 00:24:17.860
Or, like, something happens faster or something happens slower.

00:24:17.960 --> 00:24:19.900
But, like, it felt like it was always, like, you see someone.

00:24:20.200 --> 00:24:23.900
Now you have a couple seconds or at least a full second, maybe two,

00:24:24.060 --> 00:24:26.640
to just hit the shoot button and then you win.

00:24:26.740 --> 00:24:30.180
Yeah, well, on the harder difficulties, they get much faster.

00:24:31.220 --> 00:24:35.320
One thing I noticed, though, is that you can't ever really shoot the hostages.

00:24:35.340 --> 00:24:39.360
Maybe they start killing them if you take too long.

00:24:39.360 --> 00:24:41.640
But then maybe—but I don't really—I don't know.

00:24:42.740 --> 00:24:48.500
Because that would make the timer kind of not fit with the story of the game.

00:24:49.080 --> 00:24:51.900
But I was never able to accidentally shoot a hostage.

00:24:52.080 --> 00:24:57.920
But I have been in situations in this game where I got shot or my guy got shot,

00:24:57.960 --> 00:24:59.300
so I'd have to use a new one.

00:25:00.880 --> 00:25:03.540
So, yeah, it does change.

00:25:03.540 --> 00:25:08.980
It implies that the hostages won't die if you shoot your gun,

00:25:09.100 --> 00:25:10.100
but they will get injured.

00:25:10.100 --> 00:25:13.020
And I'm not sure if that affects your ending or not.

00:25:13.160 --> 00:25:16.020
Because there are other endings in this game.

00:25:16.300 --> 00:25:18.840
Yeah, I have gotten, like, the—

00:25:19.400 --> 00:25:24.460
I did finish one of the harder difficulties with a death,

00:25:24.480 --> 00:25:30.260
and it said, like, yeah, the mission's over, but it wasn't the best success.

00:25:30.260 --> 00:25:31.720
Right. Okay, got it.

00:25:31.840 --> 00:25:35.500
Yeah. Because there's, like, there's success conditions for, like,

00:25:35.520 --> 00:25:40.060
if all the hostages are safe, but one of your soldiers died.

00:25:40.220 --> 00:25:43.160
There's obviously the opposite if all your soldiers survive,

00:25:43.180 --> 00:25:47.340
but you happen to also kill two hostages or injure two hostages, blah, blah, blah.

00:25:47.500 --> 00:25:48.320
That's not great.

00:25:48.620 --> 00:25:51.460
And then the last one is 100% success.

00:25:51.720 --> 00:25:54.520
No fatal deaths except for the terrorists, of course.

00:25:54.520 --> 00:25:58.660
And the terrorists are green and the hostages are blue.

00:25:58.840 --> 00:26:02.520
So it's not, like, too complicated, I suppose,

00:26:02.720 --> 00:26:04.460
especially because it's their entire bodies.

00:26:05.080 --> 00:26:07.860
They're completely covered in either green or blue,

00:26:07.860 --> 00:26:09.220
and you're blue yourself.

00:26:09.560 --> 00:26:14.500
So it's not necessarily, like, the fast twitch reflexes of, like, Hogan's Alley

00:26:14.500 --> 00:26:17.800
where they had the cardboard come by,

00:26:17.820 --> 00:26:19.460
and you have to, like—

00:26:19.460 --> 00:26:21.600
You're like, I don't know which one's going to be the hostage

00:26:21.600 --> 00:26:22.580
and which one's going to be the killer.

00:26:22.580 --> 00:26:24.660
It's like you kind of—you see one or the other.

00:26:24.800 --> 00:26:27.460
You either see the hostage or you see the terrorist.

00:26:28.960 --> 00:26:33.380
Yeah. And again, when you see a hostage, it's like,

00:26:33.460 --> 00:26:35.140
oh, okay, I'm glad you're here.

00:26:36.540 --> 00:26:40.760
Nothing can happen. Just like in real life.

00:26:41.040 --> 00:26:44.260
Yep. And so that's pretty much the game.

00:26:44.480 --> 00:26:46.960
Like I said, you can change the difficulty

00:26:46.960 --> 00:26:48.460
or change the amount of time,

00:26:49.960 --> 00:26:52.700
but, you know, unless all six of your guys die

00:26:52.700 --> 00:26:53.940
or you run out of time,

00:26:54.420 --> 00:26:57.460
you will either win or lose the game based on those conditions.

00:26:58.300 --> 00:27:01.080
I don't think you can, like, win the game.

00:27:01.220 --> 00:27:03.160
Like, as soon as all three of your soldiers die,

00:27:03.260 --> 00:27:07.040
that's like it. You can't really do much from the sniping POV, correct?

00:27:07.480 --> 00:27:10.200
You couldn't win the game by taking out all the guys from the windows.

00:27:10.200 --> 00:27:12.960
No, you can just sort of thin the herd that way.

00:27:13.140 --> 00:27:14.680
Yeah, and I guess, again,

00:27:14.820 --> 00:27:17.780
this is an instance where it would probably feel different

00:27:17.780 --> 00:27:18.880
if I played on a higher difficulty,

00:27:18.880 --> 00:27:21.900
but it really just felt like the sniping minigame

00:27:21.900 --> 00:27:23.260
didn't really do that much

00:27:23.260 --> 00:27:27.000
because it was so easy to do everything else without the sniping.

00:27:27.080 --> 00:27:28.080
It felt like it took up time.

00:27:28.420 --> 00:27:31.260
I mean, I could only ever find one guy in those windows anyways,

00:27:31.260 --> 00:27:33.340
so I shot that guy and I looked forever,

00:27:33.560 --> 00:27:34.820
couldn't find him. I was like, I'm just going to move on.

00:27:34.940 --> 00:27:36.380
I think you might have been dealing, though, Joe,

00:27:36.600 --> 00:27:38.240
just with the difficulty there,

00:27:38.240 --> 00:27:40.460
is there are less enemies, you know,

00:27:40.560 --> 00:27:44.100
so I think that you would have seen more in the windows potentially

00:27:44.100 --> 00:27:46.640
if you're playing on a higher difficulty.

00:27:46.720 --> 00:27:53.060
That makes sense. On the sequels and spinoff side,

00:27:53.300 --> 00:27:57.100
this game has a story behind it,

00:27:57.160 --> 00:27:58.640
but not necessarily an interesting one.

00:27:58.640 --> 00:28:00.060
It's just a bunch of name changes.

00:28:00.300 --> 00:28:04.000
So this game came out in arcades as hostages,

00:28:04.700 --> 00:28:09.220
then came out on computers as hostage rescue mission,

00:28:09.240 --> 00:28:12.800
and then was changed for the NES,

00:28:13.300 --> 00:28:14.500
just the NES, keep in mind,

00:28:14.840 --> 00:28:17.140
to rescue the embassy mission.

00:28:17.660 --> 00:28:21.240
I kind of like hostage rescue mission,

00:28:21.240 --> 00:28:23.180
but then I start thinking about, like,

00:28:24.040 --> 00:28:25.600
you know, is it unclear?

00:28:25.600 --> 00:28:28.360
Like, are you one of the hostages now trying to rescue

00:28:29.020 --> 00:28:32.180
the other people that are being held hostage?

00:28:32.420 --> 00:28:34.740
Like, that's what hostage rescue mission read to me.

00:28:34.920 --> 00:28:37.960
Yeah, I guess if there's a colon after hostage,

00:28:38.180 --> 00:28:40.420
I could see that it should just be hostage rescue mission.

00:28:40.620 --> 00:28:41.940
Right, right, and there is a colon.

00:28:42.180 --> 00:28:45.960
That is a mission to rescue a hostage. I like that it just gets more and more specific

00:28:46.720 --> 00:28:48.040
as the changes are made.

00:28:48.740 --> 00:28:52.620
Yes. One thing that I wanted to note just about the developer

00:28:52.620 --> 00:28:54.240
is I thought it was funny that, like,

00:28:55.020 --> 00:28:56.800
not funny, but interesting that we got, like,

00:28:56.800 --> 00:28:58.160
like, an Infogrames game,

00:28:59.240 --> 00:29:02.220
because I only know them as, like,

00:29:02.360 --> 00:29:05.120
they're the ones that made Alone in the Dark,

00:29:05.300 --> 00:29:07.480
which sort of was the grandfather

00:29:07.480 --> 00:29:10.520
of all 3D survival horror games,

00:29:11.360 --> 00:29:13.860
and, like, really nothing else of note

00:29:13.860 --> 00:29:15.560
that they were always kind of like

00:29:15.560 --> 00:29:17.280
a middle-of-the-road developer,

00:29:17.760 --> 00:29:20.240
but French, which I guess explains, like,

00:29:20.320 --> 00:29:21.820
the Paris angle here.

00:29:22.000 --> 00:29:23.300
I just wanted to note that,

00:29:23.380 --> 00:29:25.880
which is, it just tickled me, I guess.

00:29:25.880 --> 00:29:28.480
Yeah, I never played Alone in the Dark,

00:29:28.560 --> 00:29:30.680
but, and I didn't know that they were responsible for it,

00:29:30.740 --> 00:29:31.760
but I know that logo.

00:29:32.740 --> 00:29:34.120
It's, like, a weird, first off.

00:29:34.120 --> 00:29:34.940
Yeah, the loop-de-loops.

00:29:35.120 --> 00:29:35.920
Yeah, the loop-de-loops,

00:29:36.020 --> 00:29:40.260
but Infogrames, or Info, yeah, Grames, right?

00:29:40.640 --> 00:29:41.880
I don't know how to pronounce it.

00:29:42.000 --> 00:29:44.020
It's French. Infogrames, I don't know,

00:29:44.060 --> 00:29:45.140
that just, like, always looked,

00:29:45.320 --> 00:29:49.160
as a, you know, strictly American kid,

00:29:49.180 --> 00:29:50.940
it always looked like a spelling mistake

00:29:50.940 --> 00:29:52.160
for Infogames.

00:29:52.160 --> 00:29:54.720
The R just felt like it shouldn't have been there,

00:29:54.760 --> 00:29:55.860
but what about, you know,

00:29:55.940 --> 00:29:57.600
I wasn't worldly back then,

00:29:57.600 --> 00:29:59.460
so that's my experience with thinking about

00:29:59.460 --> 00:30:00.960
that developer.

00:30:01.320 --> 00:30:05.220
They would make a sequel called Alcatraz,

00:30:05.960 --> 00:30:06.780
but it was mostly a sequel

00:30:06.780 --> 00:30:08.720
to the Arcade Hostages game,

00:30:08.720 --> 00:30:10.340
so I'm not sure, like,

00:30:10.760 --> 00:30:12.300
how much was changed or whatever,

00:30:12.480 --> 00:30:14.800
but it only ever came out on computers,

00:30:14.800 --> 00:30:17.040
like the Amiga and DOS systems

00:30:17.040 --> 00:30:18.000
and stuff like that,

00:30:18.000 --> 00:30:20.820
so would you play Alcatraz?

00:30:20.940 --> 00:30:22.100
Would you like to see what they update?

00:30:22.660 --> 00:30:23.640
Yeah, let's do it.

00:30:24.280 --> 00:30:25.720
Sure, let's just do it.

00:30:25.940 --> 00:30:28.140
Let's just start an Amiga podcast.

00:30:28.140 --> 00:30:29.140
I heard there's only, like,

00:30:29.260 --> 00:30:31.160
3,000 games for the Amiga,

00:30:31.180 --> 00:30:33.360
so we can get started right away.

00:30:35.400 --> 00:30:37.620
It says, The plot Alcatraz Island,

00:30:37.900 --> 00:30:40.200
once home to America's most undesirable criminals,

00:30:40.420 --> 00:30:41.540
lies empty and abandoned.

00:30:41.740 --> 00:30:42.640
Since its closure,

00:30:42.640 --> 00:30:44.560
it was used as a tourist destination.

00:30:44.560 --> 00:30:47.260
Now, it is used by the notorious criminal,

00:30:47.560 --> 00:30:48.580
Miguel Tardiez.

00:30:50.500 --> 00:30:52.800
To use as the center of his distribution network

00:30:52.800 --> 00:30:55.580
for his drug and gun running and money laundering.

00:30:55.660 --> 00:30:56.540
How many things is he doing?

00:30:56.580 --> 00:30:58.800
He's doing a distribution network,

00:30:59.000 --> 00:31:00.600
a drug ring, a gun ring,

00:31:00.760 --> 00:31:02.360
and money laundering.

00:31:02.980 --> 00:31:06.140
So, learning that Tardiez is on American soil,

00:31:06.420 --> 00:31:09.640
the U.S. Navy SEALs dispatch their best operatives,

00:31:09.920 --> 00:31:11.080
Bird and Fist,

00:31:11.080 --> 00:31:15.380
their mission to covertly breach the disused prison,

00:31:15.620 --> 00:31:17.820
destroy the drug and weapons caches,

00:31:18.040 --> 00:31:19.720
and apprehend Tardiez.

00:31:20.320 --> 00:31:23.040
I like it better just as, like,

00:31:23.180 --> 00:31:24.580
oh, there's somebody at the embassy,

00:31:24.820 --> 00:31:27.000
make it stop. Yeah, this feels much more involved.

00:31:27.160 --> 00:31:30.200
It doesn't even, I guess I must play similarly,

00:31:30.200 --> 00:31:32.900
but it feels like it has a lot more going on

00:31:32.900 --> 00:31:34.040
and probably has, like,

00:31:34.680 --> 00:31:37.280
you know, individual levels and stuff like that.

00:31:38.000 --> 00:31:40.160
Who could say? The only play is the two guys?

00:31:40.160 --> 00:31:41.800
Whatever. Yeah, just, yeah, exactly.

00:31:41.940 --> 00:31:43.540
We were playing a six in this one.

00:31:43.680 --> 00:31:46.020
That's a demake, in my opinion.

00:31:47.140 --> 00:31:49.760
And we have to give our opinion on one more thing, guys,

00:31:49.940 --> 00:31:52.780
and that is whether Rescue, the embassy mission,

00:31:53.000 --> 00:31:55.360
is on the Essential Games list.

00:31:58.300 --> 00:32:01.600
I still think that there's a lot of neat stuff

00:32:01.600 --> 00:32:02.620
going on in this game.

00:32:02.620 --> 00:32:04.920
It is a fast game.

00:32:04.920 --> 00:32:07.300
It is something you could experience no matter what,

00:32:07.320 --> 00:32:09.200
so it is kind of just worth checking out.

00:32:09.200 --> 00:32:10.840
I guess I just wasn't expecting,

00:32:11.240 --> 00:32:12.420
you know, even if you look at, like,

00:32:12.480 --> 00:32:14.540
we didn't talk about this, but when you start up the game,

00:32:14.700 --> 00:32:16.280
if you just don't press anything,

00:32:16.640 --> 00:32:18.260
you're greeted to some pretty cool, like,

00:32:18.900 --> 00:32:21.600
pixel art cut scenes and stuff like that.

00:32:21.600 --> 00:32:23.500
And even, like, when you're playing as the sniper

00:32:23.500 --> 00:32:27.780
and you're going for the guys in the window,

00:32:27.780 --> 00:32:30.620
like, it's not just some crosshairs view

00:32:30.620 --> 00:32:32.400
with a bunch of, you know, lines

00:32:32.400 --> 00:32:34.000
and you just shoot silhouettes.

00:32:34.000 --> 00:32:35.320
It has a nice, like,

00:32:35.640 --> 00:32:37.760
it gives a nice contextual to everything

00:32:37.760 --> 00:32:39.120
that's going on in the game,

00:32:39.900 --> 00:32:42.120
except for the idea that you can only see

00:32:42.120 --> 00:32:43.540
straight ahead in a room

00:32:43.540 --> 00:32:45.780
and that we all have to turn four ways

00:32:45.780 --> 00:32:47.400
to see everything inside of a room.

00:32:47.500 --> 00:32:48.320
I don't know about you guys,

00:32:48.460 --> 00:32:50.420
but I can see a little more

00:32:50.420 --> 00:32:51.640
than just straight ahead.

00:32:53.060 --> 00:32:54.600
So all that to say that, like,

00:32:54.700 --> 00:32:57.160
while it's doing a lot of interesting things,

00:32:57.180 --> 00:32:58.520
and we've been seeing that a lot

00:32:58.520 --> 00:33:01.600
with 1990s NES games trying to shake things up,

00:33:01.600 --> 00:33:03.060
sometimes when you shake things up,

00:33:03.080 --> 00:33:04.680
it's not necessarily for the better.

00:33:04.860 --> 00:33:06.280
It's just to stand out a little bit.

00:33:06.440 --> 00:33:09.380
I think this is a game that I would have bought.

00:33:10.100 --> 00:33:12.340
It would have been, like, different

00:33:12.340 --> 00:33:15.300
and salesy enough with its, like, you know,

00:33:15.360 --> 00:33:18.740
rescue these hostages by being this elite team of six

00:33:18.740 --> 00:33:21.820
and you have both the snipers and the soldiers.

00:33:22.060 --> 00:33:23.960
Like, you know, I think there's a lot going on in this game.

00:33:23.960 --> 00:33:25.400
I just don't know if I would have actually

00:33:25.400 --> 00:33:27.820
been satisfied with my purchase

00:33:28.900 --> 00:33:31.480
after finding out that that's all there is to the game.

00:33:31.960 --> 00:33:33.020
So for that reason,

00:33:33.200 --> 00:33:34.680
it's not on the essential games list,

00:33:35.260 --> 00:33:37.160
but I wouldn't say that I, you know,

00:33:37.280 --> 00:33:39.140
like, it's a lot of fun

00:33:39.140 --> 00:33:41.300
for the small amount of time that it is.

00:33:41.420 --> 00:33:43.260
So in today's day where you're just, like,

00:33:43.720 --> 00:33:45.320
downloading mobile games all the time

00:33:45.320 --> 00:33:48.000
and playing NES games on emulators,

00:33:48.460 --> 00:33:51.300
you'd really, like, you'd be fooling yourself

00:33:51.300 --> 00:33:52.940
if you didn't think it was worth checking out

00:33:52.940 --> 00:33:55.600
just because it's such a short experience to understand.

00:33:56.020 --> 00:33:58.480
Sean? Yeah, I kind of agree with you in that.

00:33:58.480 --> 00:34:00.680
I probably would have bought this game

00:34:00.680 --> 00:34:03.900
had I been around to do so

00:34:03.900 --> 00:34:07.740
because it sort of ticked it on the surface.

00:34:08.440 --> 00:34:11.540
It looks to tick a lot of the boxes that I want.

00:34:12.220 --> 00:34:15.500
I think, though, one of its greatest sins

00:34:15.500 --> 00:34:17.620
is tricking me into thinking

00:34:17.620 --> 00:34:19.120
it's a different game than it is.

00:34:19.300 --> 00:34:22.540
Like, just a quick look at that map screen

00:34:22.540 --> 00:34:26.080
that's sort of showing the, like, where you are

00:34:26.080 --> 00:34:29.500
and it looks very tactical

00:34:30.380 --> 00:34:32.700
while you're running up on the embassy

00:34:32.700 --> 00:34:36.140
and then when you're sort of deciding

00:34:36.140 --> 00:34:39.500
whether you're going to snipe or repel

00:34:39.500 --> 00:34:42.840
and you get to place guys where you want.

00:34:42.960 --> 00:34:44.260
None of those things really matter.

00:34:44.580 --> 00:34:45.980
Like, it doesn't matter where you place them.

00:34:46.120 --> 00:34:48.500
You're just going to go to the third, second, or first floor.

00:34:48.880 --> 00:34:54.880
It looks so much more like a procedural strategy game.

00:34:54.880 --> 00:34:57.820
Not procedural in the modern sense

00:34:57.820 --> 00:35:00.880
but, like, a systems-based strategy game

00:35:00.880 --> 00:35:04.500
where you can get creative with the way that you infiltrate

00:35:04.500 --> 00:35:07.380
and prepare in different ways.

00:35:08.280 --> 00:35:09.580
That's not what this is.

00:35:09.740 --> 00:35:12.200
This is three or four minigames

00:35:12.200 --> 00:35:15.340
one of which is a bit more robust than the others

00:35:16.880 --> 00:35:18.360
with some cool art.

00:35:18.660 --> 00:35:19.920
I didn't hate it.

00:35:19.920 --> 00:35:23.960
I think that each of them, like, are good for what they do

00:35:23.960 --> 00:35:28.480
and how everything contextually fits together is nice.

00:35:29.080 --> 00:35:33.160
But I can't play this game without thinking about

00:35:33.160 --> 00:35:35.500
what I originally thought it was

00:35:35.500 --> 00:35:41.100
and that's a sin that I can't allow them to atone for.

00:35:41.380 --> 00:35:45.460
So, yeah, maybe play it and get tricked like I did

00:35:45.460 --> 00:35:47.720
but also not essential.

00:35:47.720 --> 00:35:51.800
Joe. I'd say it feels like, to me,

00:35:51.960 --> 00:35:55.620
this game succeeded in what it seems like

00:35:55.620 --> 00:35:56.940
it was trying to do.

00:35:57.340 --> 00:35:59.060
And what I mean by that is that, like,

00:35:59.060 --> 00:36:03.520
it feels like it meant to be this short little experience

00:36:03.520 --> 00:36:06.180
with, like, these mini-game elements

00:36:06.180 --> 00:36:09.380
that tie together this mission that you're on.

00:36:09.660 --> 00:36:13.040
And I think that in 1990, I agree,

00:36:13.120 --> 00:36:17.000
this would have been an interesting, unique, fun experience.

00:36:17.000 --> 00:36:18.620
I feel like it would be, like, even in 1990,

00:36:18.620 --> 00:36:20.080
I feel like it would be, like, the equivalent

00:36:20.080 --> 00:36:22.040
of some quirky, weird, like,

00:36:23.400 --> 00:36:25.720
one or two sitting Steam game or something,

00:36:25.860 --> 00:36:26.720
which, like, is fun.

00:36:27.640 --> 00:36:30.360
But, like, it never gets to a point where I'm like,

00:36:30.440 --> 00:36:32.320
oh, yeah. Even in 1990, I can't...

00:36:33.080 --> 00:36:34.980
I'm not sure I would tell people

00:36:34.980 --> 00:36:36.880
this is on my essential games list.

00:36:37.480 --> 00:36:38.880
I think I would say, yeah, it was a fun.

00:36:38.900 --> 00:36:42.620
It was a fun little experience.

00:36:42.620 --> 00:36:46.540
But essential back then, I'd probably say no,

00:36:46.660 --> 00:36:47.720
and now certainly not.

00:36:47.980 --> 00:36:49.580
So check it out.

00:36:49.880 --> 00:36:51.640
It's kind of cool, but definitely not essential.

